  <RESPONSE>
    There's no single canonical "best" config, but the field has converged on some well-established patterns:
    
    ---
    
    ## General Routing Philosophy
    
    Route at runtime, not at design time. The routing decision is often worth more than picking the "best" single model. A well-tuned routing setup cuts costs 50–80% with minimal quality loss.
    
    ---
    
    ## Task Tier Framework (most common approach)
    
    | Tier | Models | Tasks |
    |---|---|---|
    | Tiny/fast | Haiku, Gemma, local 7B | File nav, boilerplate, classification, linting, simple Q&A |
    | Mid | Sonnet, Mistral, DeepSeek V3 | Code gen, summarization, structured extraction |
    | Large | Opus, GPT-4o, local 70B | Multi-step reasoning, coordination, novel problems |
    
    The key insight from the Augment Code routing guide: most prompts in an agent session are *simple* — imports, docstrings, rename ops — and routing those to Haiku vs Opus gives identical output at ~5x lower cost.
    
    ---
    
    ## Cascade vs. Router
    
    - **Router**: classify the prompt upfront → send to one model
    - **Cascade**: try small model first → escalate if confidence is low
    
    Cascade is better when you can't predict difficulty beforehand. [This arxiv paper](https://arxiv.org/pdf/2410.10347) gives a unified treatment of both approaches.
    
    ---
    
    ## Practical Tools
    
    - **[RouteLLM](https://github.com/lm-sys/routellm)** (LMSYS) — OpenAI-compatible server, trains classifiers on preference data; `mf` (matrix factorization) router is their recommended default
    - **[Not Diamond](https://github.com/Not-Diamond/awesome-ai-model-routing)** — commercial, predicts best model per prompt; has a curated list of routing research
    - **[LiteLLM](https://litellm.ai)** — proxy that unifies 100+ models, supports fallback chains and cost-based routing
    - **[LLMRouter](https://github.com/ulab-uiuc/LLMRouter)** — newer (Dec 2025), 16+ router strategies, unified CLI
    
    ---
    
    ## Local Model Integration
    
    By 2026, local models (Gemma 4, DeepSeek V3/V4, MiniMax) are genuinely viable for tier-1 and tier-2 tasks. The cost delta is massive: ~$0.0001/M tokens local vs $5–30/M for cloud. The practical pattern is:
    
    > **Local model → Haiku → Sonnet → Opus**, escalating on failure/low confidence
    
    The main friction with local routing is that learned routers need retraining when models change — a real operational cost. Rule-based routing (complexity heuristics, token count, task type tags) is more robust for most teams.
    
    ---
    
    **Bottom line:** For a Claude-centric stack, the most practical starting point is **LiteLLM** as a proxy with a fallback chain (Haiku → Sonnet → Opus), with a local model (Ollama/vLLM) as a tier-0 for trivially simple ops. Add RouteLLM's classifier on top if you want learned routing with quality guarantees.
    
    Sources:
    - [Augment Code AI Model Routing Guide](https://www.augmentcode.com/guides/ai-model-routing-guide)
    - [RouteLLM (GitHub)](https://github.com/lm-sys/routellm)
    - [Not Diamond – Awesome AI Model Routing](https://github.com/Not-Diamond/awesome-ai-model-routing)
    - [A Unified Approach to Routing and Cascading for LLMs (arxiv)](https://arxiv.org/pdf/2410.10347)
    - [LLMRouter (GitHub)](https://github.com/ulab-uiuc/LLMRouter)
    - [Top 5 AI Gateways for Multi-Model Routing 2026](https://www.getmaxim.ai/articles/top-5-ai-gateways-for-multi-model-routing-in-2026/)
